WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.979 --> 00:00:31.009
If you are not intending to listen to a panel
on social

00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:51.570
commerce you're in the wrong place. We have
actually a

00:00:51.570 --> 00:01:00.020
really great group of speakers here who represent
a lot of

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.620
different aspects of social commerce and so
what I wanted to

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.520
was start off by - let's start off with Brian,
at the end

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:14.010
there, who is one of the founders of AirBNB
and is

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.170
definitely in the anti-portfolio for floodgate.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:22.920
We actually heard the pitch for AirBNB right
before they

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:29.050
went live in the democratic convention. Whenever
I'm asked

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:36.250
which company I regret not investing in, the
number one

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:41.740
answer for me is AirBNB. Bring on the pain.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.700
Is it just introduction?

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:49.930
Yes, introduction. Tell us a little bit about
your company

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:53.690
and a little about your background.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:02:00.400
Excellent. Yes, I'm Brian Chesky. I'm one
of the founders

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:11.060
and CEO of www.AirBNB.com. We are a community
market place

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:18.360
where you can list and book all sorts of unique
spaces

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:24.340
around the world; like to say anything from
a private

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:35.900
apartment to a private island. It's all sorts
of spaces from

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.349
a night to a year. We are in 10000 cities.

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:44.689
Travelers came last year from every country
on a plane up in

00:02:44.689 --> 00:02:50.620
North Korea and the Congo, so we're literally
in 192

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:56.249
countries around the world and we really started
in New York

00:02:56.249 --> 00:03:02.290
City. We started getting a lot of traction
about two years

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:08.950
ago and it has really taken off since then.
We are in about

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:16.140
10000 cities and this is a business that was
somewhat

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.019
serendipitously started.

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:29.290
I had moved up from Los Angeles to San Francisco
about three

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.109
years ago and I was with my college roommate
at the time and

00:03:31.109 --> 00:03:40.150
we weren't able to make rent and a design
conference was

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:46.269
coming to San Francisco. All the hotels for
design

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:52.459
conference was sold out and I had originally
quit my job,

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:58.389
and I think I had $1000 in the bank account.

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:09.629
This is October 2007, so probably a really
good planning,

00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:15.870
obviously. I get to San Francisco and I realize
rent was

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:22.600
$1150, so I already had enough problem right
there. We didn'

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:37.030
t know what to do. This conference was coming
to San

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:43.760
Francisco, all the hotels were sold out. We
had this idea,

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.880
why don't we create a bed &amp; breakfast for
the conference?

00:04:47.880 --> 00:05:00.900
That was the vision. There was no business
plan; the vision

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:14.550
was let's make rent for the month of October.
That was the

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:34.760
business plan basically. I told Joe, "We don't
have any

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:45.950
furniture so we can't create a bed &amp; breakfast."
He said, "

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:57.900
Brian, we have a few airbeds in the closet."
We blew up some

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:10.080
airbeds, we called it the Air Bed &amp; Breakfast.
It was a

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.390
temporary bed &amp; breakfast, we ended up making
I think $1000

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:26.980
by hosting a bunch of people that week and
that was supposed

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:29.930
to be it.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:39.770
Over the next few months, people started saying
they'd love

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:52.030
to use us and that would be awesome if they
could go

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:59.080
somewhere and use it. I'm not going to go
into full story

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:06.210
except to say that we started our business
to solve our own

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:15.370
problem and our own problem was to make rent
and we'd like

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:29.090
to say what started as an airbed in the living
room is now

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:36.470
spread to 10000 cities around the world.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.580
That's Gregg.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:49.130
Hi, Gregg Fence. I'm the Chief Marketing Officer
of One

00:07:49.130 --> 00:08:01.780
Kings Lane. I joined late last year after
10 years at E-Bay

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:09.130
and a wide variety of ten years in marketing,
general

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:14.830
management and products, so quite a few things
happened over

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:39.120
the course of the 2000s and prior to that.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:48.350
I worked at Coca-Cola and kind of classic
marketing, classic

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:54.750
brand management. We are a company founded
just about two

00:08:54.750 --> 00:09:00.339
years ago by Susan Feldman and Alison Pinkus,
really focused

00:09:00.339 --> 00:09:03.519
on the home and living space and we are just
laser-focused

00:09:03.519 --> 00:09:07.730
on driving deep and delivering on the consumer
needs and

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:14.160
also the industry needs in a very fragmented
industry that's

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:19.579
been very challenged with the downturn of
the economy.

00:09:19.579 --> 00:09:26.800
We're grounded in sales events but you've
probably seen

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:33.209
overtime that we're growing into new forms
e-commerce;

00:09:33.209 --> 00:09:41.230
better ways to serve products to our consumers
and really

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:48.449
delivering a great curated branded experience
in many, many

00:09:48.449 --> 00:09:54.480
interesting ways and really trying to weave
commerce and

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:00.490
content together, tell an interesting story
and then attach

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:12.459
products around those stories which really,
I don't think,

00:10:12.459 --> 00:10:29.629
has been done. It was sort of the promise
of the internet in

00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:44.749
early 2000s. We're going to try to crack with
a deep focus

00:10:44.749 --> 00:10:48.620
on home and living.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:11:07.050
My name is Brett Garrett, Bain Capital Ventures;
which is a

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:23.540
venture and growth equity, investment on bank
capital. We're

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:40.670
deeply involved in lots of social and e-commerce
and social

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:49.249
e-commerce companies from LinkedIn to Rent-A-Runway
which is

00:11:49.249 --> 00:11:59.080
a kind of a high end dress rental, the Netflix
of dresses

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:15.490
we'd like to call that. 
The Find, which is a vertical search

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:29.990
for shopping. Buy With Me, which is a group
buying website

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:42.360
and we're looking to invest in a lot more.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:55.899
I'll be representing the VC part of the room.

00:12:55.899 --> 00:13:10.379
My name is Brett Keintz, I'm an '08 grad of
GSB and I work

00:13:10.379 --> 00:13:21.079
with Groupon. My official title is Director
of Social. I'm

00:13:21.079 --> 00:13:30.720
in the product management group and my background
is

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:46.420
primarily in social, so in building apps on
the Facebook

00:13:46.420 --> 00:14:07.350
platform, on the Twitter platform and at Groupon,
I do a lot

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:17.230
of new product development.

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:38.080
And this is our last. I'm Ann Miura-Ko, I'll
be moderating

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:45.350
this session. I am an investor at Floodgate
and I've

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:57.309
invested in a couple of social commerce companies.
In our

00:14:57.309 --> 00:15:07.110
portfolio, we have a company called Chegg,
which is textbook

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:17.720
rentals and I'm also on the board of ModCloth,
which is a

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:23.809
good example of social commerce in the fashion
space.

00:15:23.809 --> 00:15:35.559
I wanted to kick off this conversation. There
is a lot of

00:15:35.559 --> 00:15:43.439
different opinions as to what social commerce
means.

00:15:43.439 --> 00:15:53.300
Everyone talks about social commerce but they
seem to be

00:15:53.300 --> 00:16:38.110
talking about it in a lot of different ways.
I wanted to go

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:51.399
down this group and just tell us from your
perspective what

00:16:51.399 --> 00:16:59.459
are some of the defining characteristics of
social commerce.

00:16:59.459 --> 00:17:07.140
Social commerce is definitely a big buzzword
these days. I

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:30.710
think the way I think about it 
and the way I think we think

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:56.029
about at Groupon is a lot of the way that
Facebook defines

00:17:56.029 --> 00:18:11.130
sort of this concept of a graph, a lot of
people will say, "

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:21.580
You throw Facebook connect on a website and
you've got

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:35.600
social commerce," that's not really fundamentally
how it

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:53.380
works. It's not just about virality and sharing
through the

00:18:53.380 --> 00:19:08.690
newsfeed to your friends whenever you buy
something, that's

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:24.519
not truly social. I think you have to figure
what your

00:19:24.519 --> 00:19:35.559
business is and what the nodes of
[inaudible] business are.

00:19:35.559 --> 00:19:48.070
For us, it's local businesses and consumers
and how we

00:19:48.070 --> 00:19:56.779
connect those two through commerce, through
a transaction.

00:19:56.779 --> 00:20:46.000
That's how I really think about social commerce
and

00:20:46.000 --> 00:21:04.570
permissions play a big part of that. The permission
of how

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:17.750
you can engage with the consumer, how you
can get them to be

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:25.070
open to receiving messages or want to receive
these sort of

00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:31.730
messages, that's really where Facebook has
done the best out

00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:37.440
of anyone in enabling this two-way communication
chAnnl

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:39.070
between friends.

00:21:39.070 --> 00:21:47.230
The same thing applies for local businesses.
It applies to

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:55.120
home manufacturers, it applies to people who
have an

00:21:55.120 --> 00:22:04.851
apartment or an air bed and breakfast that
they want to

00:22:04.851 --> 00:22:15.500
connect with other consumers. That's kind
of how I think

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:17.970
about it.

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:29.280
I think I would agree with that. I think in
general social

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:38.260
e-commerce is a pretty unyouthful label because
it is so

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:47.860
broad and so undefined, but I think within
it there are

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:55.660
really interesting businesses and there are
really

00:22:55.660 --> 00:23:00.730
uninteresting businesses in terms what social
commerce is

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:18.240
and so, I think there is one social commerce
where you just

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:35.530
take your business and you have Facebook connect
and now

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:52.230
social commerce and there is another way you're
really doing

00:23:52.230 --> 00:24:05.600
something new; you're either taking your social
activities

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:18.710
offline, you're facilitating going to the
Web somehow or

00:24:18.710 --> 00:24:24.570
you're creating totally new behaviors like
AirBNB, they are

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:34.899
only possible because of the Web and you're
able to kind of

00:24:34.899 --> 00:24:46.990
create a commerce platform that way with social
connections.

00:24:46.990 --> 00:24:52.480
I think in that spectrum, I think the more
interesting ones

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:59.200
are the ones where you're not just sticking
some kind of

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:05.559
Facebook connect or some social label on something
that you'

00:25:05.559 --> 00:25:08.280
re already doing but you're really imagining
something very

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:15.259
new and something that's available to do only
now because

00:25:15.259 --> 00:25:27.919
there is a social graph that you can leverage,
so I make

00:25:27.919 --> 00:25:30.370
that distinction.

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:41.200
Same opinion. I think the basics is wiring
your setup to

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:47.140
share is really just the basics but how we're
thinking about

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:57.799
things is how do consumers really start influencing
our

00:25:57.799 --> 00:26:15.049
shelves in the realtime, how does our inventory
shift as our

00:26:15.049 --> 00:26:30.730
consumer needs shift around that? How do we
really co-opt

00:26:30.730 --> 00:26:43.279
our consumers into becoming our sales force,
our

00:26:43.279 --> 00:26:57.389
merchandising team or customer support team?
How do you

00:26:57.389 --> 00:27:09.539
incent them, how do you arm them, how do you
encourage them

00:27:09.539 --> 00:27:15.899
authentically to go to work on your behalf
because they love

00:27:15.899 --> 00:27:17.299
your brand?

00:27:17.299 --> 00:27:22.769
I don't think companies have really done that
well

00:27:22.769 --> 00:27:25.899
authentically. That's the magic word it's
scale

00:27:25.899 --> 00:27:28.990
authentically, cost-effective and where there
has been a big

00:27:28.990 --> 00:27:34.280
fixation for all the reasons in the Facebook
and Twitter,

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:44.220
because of the network effects they've created.
We're

00:27:44.220 --> 00:28:01.629
equally as interested in the tales of social
media. Where do

00:28:01.629 --> 00:28:17.450
enthusiasts live? The Web, the forums, the
blogs, the places

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:33.519
where they talk about what they love and I
think we're going

00:28:33.519 --> 00:28:46.019
to see more folks starting to push out there
with new

00:28:46.019 --> 00:28:57.460
technologies to really try to scale out into
the tales of

00:28:57.460 --> 00:29:24.950
social media because of the enormous power
out there.

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:37.440
I think it's the early days quite honestly,
a lot of fun

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:41.930
stuff is going to happen over the next several
years.

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:47.470
I think we think of social commerce as very
distinct at

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:53.050
[inaudible] to be and it means breathing online
- offline.

00:29:53.050 --> 00:30:01.860
We feel like a lot of things before AirBNB
meant bringing

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:08.340
offline online and we think AirBNB is very
much bringing

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:13.370
online offline. We are now connection millions
of people

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:20.549
around the world. It's actually really cool
because we can

00:30:20.549 --> 00:30:28.780
create a world map and show which countries
we've connected

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:31.769
to other countries.

00:30:31.769 --> 00:30:44.909
In America, we're connected with residents
in 90 different

00:30:44.909 --> 00:30:56.600
countries last year. I believe Australians
are connected

00:30:56.600 --> 00:31:12.669
with people in 60 different countries and
we can country by

00:31:12.669 --> 00:31:18.929
country and look. If you bring those all together,
almost

00:31:18.929 --> 00:31:24.330
every single country has been connected with
another country

00:31:24.330 --> 00:31:30.429
on the planet minus the two countries I mentioned
and it's

00:31:30.429 --> 00:31:36.330
very intimate. Social commerce in our sense
is very

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:37.330
intimate.

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:45.330
There is three really intimate things that
you can do online

00:31:45.330 --> 00:31:50.529
or offline. The first thing is that you're
paying somebody

00:31:50.529 --> 00:31:58.159
else money. A financial transaction is a very
intimate

00:31:58.159 --> 00:32:04.940
transaction, because you're paying money.
Another thing you'

00:32:04.940 --> 00:32:12.769
re doing is you're meeting people in real
life that you don'

00:32:12.769 --> 00:32:30.480
t know. It's the idea of meeting somebody
in the real world

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:48.850
that you met on the internet; ten years ago
there was a

00:32:48.850 --> 00:32:59.700
stigma to that, I think it's pretty accepted
today.

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:11.169
We're combining that with money, we're writing
a third thing

00:33:11.169 --> 00:33:25.889
and the third thing is now you're in the home
with the

00:33:25.889 --> 00:33:37.820
person. If you combine those three things,
that is

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:43.240
incredibly unique kind of intimate transaction.

00:33:43.240 --> 00:33:57.029
I think that's a great point. I think one
of the customers

00:33:57.029 --> 00:34:04.090
for one of our portfolio companies Zimride
said, "My life

00:34:04.090 --> 00:34:11.929
becomes more interesting when I open my door."
I thought

00:34:11.929 --> 00:34:20.419
that really captured this notion of what a
lot of people are

00:34:20.419 --> 00:34:27.349
calling collaborative commerce or the sharing
economy.

00:34:27.349 --> 00:34:40.230
What I'd love to do is dive a little more
deeply into that

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:50.040
concept Brian around. What is it about today
that makes this

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:57.000
kind of sharing and collaborative culture
possible?

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:06.480
We've been struggling with that in a sense.
When people hear

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:19.170
about our concept, it depends. I think in
2007 people heard

00:35:19.170 --> 00:35:37.030
about our concept they thought, "Are you crazy?"
That was I

00:35:37.030 --> 00:35:56.520
think what my mom said and lots of my friends
said. Of

00:35:56.520 --> 00:36:18.140
course they don't say it at the time, they
say it a year

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:34.800
later like, "Let me tell you what I really
thought." At the

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:41.140
time they were really supportive, "Oh, it's
excellent." A

00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:46.359
year ago, "I thought you were crazy."

00:36:46.359 --> 00:37:01.140
There were things that I think had to happen
on the internet

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:10.819
before you could do all those kind of things.
You could

00:37:10.819 --> 00:37:21.180
argue you had to do all those three things
in our websites

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:27.809
but do all those three things distinctly.

00:37:27.809 --> 00:37:36.339
Paying other people; that happens on E-Bay.
Before E-Bay,

00:37:36.339 --> 00:37:46.430
there was a huge stigma to doing that and
people were not

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:51.940
comfortable. People wouldn't buy anything
online, let alone

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:58.630
pay another person online, so that had to
happen.

00:37:58.630 --> 00:38:07.360
Meeting people from the internet. 15 years
ago, even ten

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:13.859
years ago, there was a huge stigma. Today,
not a big deal.

00:38:13.859 --> 00:38:17.210
And sharing space with people from online;
that happens

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:24.390
every day on Craigslist. Maybe you get your
roommate. A lot

00:38:24.390 --> 00:38:32.270
of people in school or after you graduated,
you'll find

00:38:32.270 --> 00:38:34.609
roommates on Craigslist. The average person
probably has, so

00:38:34.609 --> 00:38:42.550
AirBNB, I think in many ways just picked all
three of those

00:38:42.550 --> 00:38:48.430
and they converged them, so they are already
doing it but we

00:38:48.430 --> 00:38:56.000
had to allow all these other websites to happen
first. And

00:38:56.000 --> 00:39:02.200
then, you bring them all those unique things
together.

00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:09.780
What about trust though? What kind of issues
do you find

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:11.150
with that?

00:39:11.150 --> 00:39:20.579
I think that's one of the other reasons. I
think trust is

00:39:20.579 --> 00:39:25.849
the currency of our marketplace and the way
our site works

00:39:25.849 --> 00:39:37.450
is you can book space or stay with people
all over the world

00:39:37.450 --> 00:39:44.700
and trust is the mechanism that allows that.
We allow you to

00:39:44.700 --> 00:40:01.319
check Facebook, you can see who their friends
are. The real

00:40:01.319 --> 00:40:15.300
key though is the reviews, the transactional
based reviews.

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:20.680
After you stay with somebody, you leave a
transactionally

00:40:20.680 --> 00:40:22.020
based review.

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:27.401
That's a quantitative score, various things
about them and

00:40:27.401 --> 00:40:34.490
they leave a reciprocal review. We have 24/7
customer

00:40:34.490 --> 00:40:40.020
support and as you build this user base of
profiles with

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:43.170
reviews, you're basically building a trusted
community

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:55.700
marketplace, it's a currency that over time
- now, it's

00:40:55.700 --> 00:41:12.660
really hard to get going because no one really
wants to be

00:41:12.660 --> 00:41:26.730
the first person without a review to book
another person

00:41:26.730 --> 00:41:35.660
without a review and so, there is a long story
of why we

00:41:35.660 --> 00:41:43.020
took AirBNB took a really long time to get
going.

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:53.050
Now we have hundreds of thousands of reviews.
One of the new

00:41:53.050 --> 00:42:05.069
features we're going to coming out with is
to allow you, if

00:42:05.069 --> 00:42:15.980
you don't yet have reviews, you can self-select
and say, "I

00:42:15.980 --> 00:42:26.349
want to stay with Stanford alumni around the
world." Maybe

00:42:26.349 --> 00:42:38.790
you're in Tokyo; we can find you a Stanford
alumni that we

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:52.829
connect you to as well, so trust can come
through being

00:42:52.829 --> 00:43:29.720
earned or it can come through affinity groups
as well.

00:43:29.720 --> 00:43:41.990
So communities is a really important part
of that.

00:43:41.990 --> 00:43:48.480
I feel like that's also, in some ways with
One Kings Lane,

00:43:48.480 --> 00:43:51.329
what's really interesting is that you guys
have really

00:43:51.329 --> 00:43:56.880
focused on one particular vertical and having
a lot of

00:43:56.880 --> 00:44:10.410
credibility within that vertical and I just
want to dive a

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:19.260
little bit more deeply into that concept.
Have you guys

00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:30.910
thought about moving into other verticals
and why did you

00:44:30.910 --> 00:44:35.490
pick this one in particular?

00:44:35.490 --> 00:44:46.730
Actually, we haven't. Laser focus by the founders
was

00:44:46.730 --> 00:44:55.030
saying, "We want to drive deep and really,
truly deliver on

00:44:55.030 --> 00:45:02.730
the needs of consumers in the home and living
category,"

00:45:02.730 --> 00:45:09.700
versus I came from E-Bay where we sold everything
to

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:14.910
everybody and it's daunting, it's hard to
deliver a really

00:45:14.910 --> 00:45:21.309
rich experience in a given category if you've
got to serve

00:45:21.309 --> 00:45:28.340
50, 60, 70 categories. So we're very laser
focused on this

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:29.690
category.

00:45:29.690 --> 00:45:35.510
In large part, because it's incredibly fragmented
on the

00:45:35.510 --> 00:45:45.031
retail side. These folks really felt the pain
when the

00:45:45.031 --> 00:45:54.839
economy turned down particularly the high
end of the

00:45:54.839 --> 00:46:00.480
industry. There are brands collapsed, the
magazines

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:07.530
collapsed. They were looking for a solution
to sell their

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:13.380
goods to consumers without degrading the brand
experience

00:46:13.380 --> 00:46:21.359
and really needed a place One Kings Lane,
just like Gill

00:46:21.359 --> 00:46:35.840
Groupe has done in the apparel industry. They
need a place

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:43.099
to move their merchandise while they keep
their brand high

00:46:43.099 --> 00:46:45.970
and really well thought of.

00:46:45.970 --> 00:46:51.500
On the consumer side, it's challenging for
consumers as

00:46:51.500 --> 00:46:56.990
well. You ask most consumers what their design
tastes are

00:46:56.990 --> 00:47:02.930
and they say, "Eclectic," because they don't
know. That's

00:47:02.930 --> 00:47:09.119
basically, "I don't know what they are." And
so what they

00:47:09.119 --> 00:47:16.690
are looking for is people to help them, guide
them, curate,

00:47:16.690 --> 00:47:29.740
give them a sense of how they can build things
out one at a

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:31.829
time, make it less intimidating, really starting
to

00:47:31.829 --> 00:47:38.030
democratize the industry which by the way,
fashion was

00:47:38.030 --> 00:47:46.210
democratized ten years ago, the internet really
ripped it

00:47:46.210 --> 00:47:52.720
out in the mainstream, it didn't happen in
home. That's we'

00:47:52.720 --> 00:48:04.099
re really looking to do is make it a whole
lot less

00:48:04.099 --> 00:48:19.090
intimidating on the consumer side, the buyer
side and really

00:48:19.090 --> 00:48:28.170
be a solution to the retail side which is
challenged right

00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:29.170
now.

00:48:29.170 --> 00:48:38.090
I'd like to dive in a little bit more into
the concept of

00:48:38.090 --> 00:48:49.299
curation that you bring up, because it feels
like

00:48:49.299 --> 00:49:03.099
particularly for groups like Gilt Groupe or
One Kings Lane

00:49:03.099 --> 00:49:20.579
and I've seen this with the ModCloth; one
of the things that

00:49:20.579 --> 00:49:28.130
develops a brand is actually point of view
as to what your

00:49:28.130 --> 00:49:34.250
brand should be about and it's something that
differentiates

00:49:34.250 --> 00:49:43.230
these types of e-commerce companies from what
I call

00:49:43.230 --> 00:49:51.320
Commerce 1.0 where you're creating essentially
a database of

00:49:51.320 --> 00:50:05.400
all the products that could be sold as in
Amazon.

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:25.069
It feels like there is a strong tie between
this curation

00:50:25.069 --> 00:50:32.299
concept and social commerce. What do you think
is that tie?

00:50:32.299 --> 00:50:41.511
I think curation is the secret sauce and we
actually as a

00:50:41.511 --> 00:50:48.500
company, one of the first things I did when
I got there, the

00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:56.750
founders said, "Really bring our brand to
life, articulate

00:50:56.750 --> 00:51:15.760
it, put it on paper, dimensionalize. As we
hire new people,

00:51:15.760 --> 00:51:41.610
we get bigger, we don't want to lose sight
of who we are and

00:51:41.610 --> 00:51:55.589
what we're all about." That's kind of rare
for companies.

00:51:55.589 --> 00:52:00.109
The challenge is, coming from E-Bay was not
curated, trying

00:52:00.109 --> 00:52:05.300
to curate. That's tough. It's easier to start
with a very

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:15.119
tight experience and walk and walk and walk
and give more

00:52:15.119 --> 00:52:26.210
power to the consumer, make it more social,
allow consumers

00:52:26.210 --> 00:52:32.210
to sell to other consumers and that's what
everyone aspires

00:52:32.210 --> 00:52:42.839
to do. It's just really hard to do tight curation
and scale.

00:52:42.839 --> 00:52:54.490
That's the magic, when you make both of those
work.

00:52:54.490 --> 00:53:05.580
AirBNB is doing a phenomenal job in making
them both work,

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:17.280
that is really the solution. And I'm biased
in I think

00:53:17.280 --> 00:53:27.710
driving deep in categories, being very focused
on a narrow

00:53:27.710 --> 00:53:40.770
set of consumers and segments is a winning
solution going

00:53:40.770 --> 00:53:42.039
forward.

00:53:42.039 --> 00:53:44.660
So to challenge that notion going on to Groupon,
you guys

00:53:44.660 --> 00:53:48.770
have really expanded really, really fast and
it doesn't

00:53:48.770 --> 00:53:56.809
necessarily feel like there is really a lot
of curation. How

00:53:56.809 --> 00:54:03.859
do you think through that kind of social commerce?
Do you

00:54:03.859 --> 00:54:20.970
want to move in a more curated direction or
do you want to

00:54:20.970 --> 00:54:25.790
just provide offers to the masses?

00:54:25.790 --> 00:54:35.599
The interesting thing is it may not be as
well known but we

00:54:35.599 --> 00:54:56.400
actually do a lot of curation, so we have
an editorial staff

00:54:56.400 --> 00:55:13.480
of probably 100 or more people that are doing
write ups on a

00:55:13.480 --> 00:55:29.920
daily basis and that's just a lot content.
I think there is

00:55:29.920 --> 00:56:05.950
a stat out there about how much content is
created by

00:56:05.950 --> 00:56:21.529
editorial team and it's something like thousands
of

00:56:21.529 --> 00:56:38.140
newspapers worth on a daily basis for every
deal that we run

00:56:38.140 --> 00:56:46.450
in every city we are in.

00:56:46.450 --> 00:57:00.079
Curation is something that we value fairly
highly. The trust

00:57:00.079 --> 00:57:25.599
factor you were talking about; we are curators
of local

00:57:25.599 --> 00:57:42.730
experiences, so we have to pick out the best
ones. We use

00:57:42.730 --> 00:57:53.839
other review sites as well and look at what
the reputations

00:57:53.839 --> 00:58:05.920
of those businesses are elsewhere as well
as in discussing

00:58:05.920 --> 00:58:16.790
with them to determine just how good those
businesses are,

00:58:16.790 --> 00:58:55.730
but at the end of the day when someone buys
a groupon, they

00:58:55.730 --> 00:59:36.250
expect it to be a great experience and if
the local merchant

00:59:36.250 --> 00:59:49.440
can't deliver on that because of whatever
reason; whether

00:59:49.440 --> 01:00:00.549
it's our fault or not we suffer for that.

01:00:00.549 --> 01:00:12.500
We try to be very careful in terms of making
sure the

01:00:12.500 --> 01:00:19.560
merchants are ready for the onslaught of customers
they

01:00:19.560 --> 01:00:29.369
have, making sure that they do actually provide
good

01:00:29.369 --> 01:00:41.260
experiences, but then there is the challenge
of growth. How

01:00:41.260 --> 01:00:54.990
do you continue to grow when you're curating
so heavily? And

01:00:54.990 --> 01:01:00.819
I think that's just something that our business
and One

01:01:00.819 --> 01:01:07.670
Kings Lane and we all have to deal with as
we're trying to

01:01:07.670 --> 01:01:15.339
create a great customer experience while still
growing

01:01:15.339 --> 01:01:21.779
massively over geographies and internationally
in both

01:01:21.779 --> 01:01:22.779
categories.

01:01:22.779 --> 01:01:26.109
Everyone thought that the solution to curation
was math;

01:01:26.109 --> 01:01:35.069
algorithms that tell you to do everything
and that's where

01:01:35.069 --> 01:01:55.789
everyone went wrong. There was no personalization
and

01:01:55.789 --> 01:01:58.609
curation is really balancing the editorial
and the

01:01:58.609 --> 01:02:03.460
algorithms. You've got to get both right,
that's where the

01:02:03.460 --> 01:02:16.260
secret is going to come which is really hard
to make a math

01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:29.680
working skill and that's why in Groupon we
say is there

01:02:29.680 --> 01:02:41.690
balancing the both sides of the equation and
really doing

01:02:41.690 --> 01:02:45.289
some amazing things.

01:02:45.289 --> 01:03:03.339
I have to agree with that. I think one of
the things that I

01:03:03.339 --> 01:03:14.921
always see in entrepreneurs is that that they
- because I

01:03:14.921 --> 01:03:24.380
teach mostly in the engineering school a lot
of these

01:03:24.380 --> 01:03:35.250
students come in thinking that the computers
can solve the

01:03:35.250 --> 01:03:42.809
problems and one of the most compelling parts
of ModCloth in

01:03:42.809 --> 01:03:52.180
particular was, there was a woman there who
really

01:03:52.180 --> 01:04:06.630
understood the community and the customers
she was selling

01:04:06.630 --> 01:04:21.080
to, so I think that's definitely an important
component.

01:04:21.080 --> 01:04:33.579
Now, we've looked through three different
types of the

01:04:33.579 --> 01:04:46.250
social commerce business. I'd love to ask
Brett, in terms of

01:04:46.250 --> 01:04:54.329
when you think about investing in these types
of companies

01:04:54.329 --> 01:05:01.609
then we've looked at curation, this concept
of collaborative

01:05:01.609 --> 01:05:08.220
commerce and what makes that successful even
with these

01:05:08.220 --> 01:05:20.140
sales sites the concept of community and how
communities

01:05:20.140 --> 01:05:57.220
will also help these couponing sites. How
do you think about

01:05:57.220 --> 01:06:12.630
evaluating these types of companies? What's
the most

01:06:12.630 --> 01:06:22.440
important component for you?

01:06:22.440 --> 01:06:44.569
There is not one component that stands out
head and

01:06:44.569 --> 01:06:59.800
shoulders above everything else but I think
there are some

01:06:59.800 --> 01:07:12.690
themes that we're generally very excited about.
I think the

01:07:12.690 --> 01:07:24.290
three panelists here that are operators really
represent I

01:07:24.290 --> 01:07:30.740
think some of those themes.

01:07:30.740 --> 01:07:43.880
I'd say one of the biggest things we're excited
about in

01:07:43.880 --> 01:07:56.170
terms of social e-commerce really is kind
of what Brian said

01:07:56.170 --> 01:08:15.240
earlier, which is taking the paradigm from
bringing people

01:08:15.240 --> 01:08:35.350
on the internet to taking people off the internet.
And so,

01:08:35.350 --> 01:08:40.942
AirBNB in a lot of ways I think was unlikely
inaugural

01:08:40.942 --> 01:08:42.109
company to do this, because of all the miracles
I think

01:08:42.109 --> 01:08:44.170
Brian had to overcome, but we're seeing lots
more

01:08:44.170 --> 01:08:45.719
marketplaces that the internet enables social
commerce on

01:08:45.719 --> 01:08:48.719
but is not really on the internet in the traditional
way.

01:08:48.719 --> 01:08:54.509
Some examples of that are a company called
thredUP based in

01:08:54.509 --> 01:08:58.630
San Francisco, which enables parents to send
each other

01:08:58.630 --> 01:09:03.101
boxes of clothes when their kids outgrow them,
so it's

01:09:03.101 --> 01:09:10.960
taking a very social practice that happened
long before

01:09:10.960 --> 01:09:23.109
there was an internet. People would say, "My
2-year old, 5-

01:09:23.109 --> 01:09:34.160
year old, 7-year old just outgrew these clothes,
I'm not

01:09:34.160 --> 01:09:46.330
waiting for an in-law or someone else to give
them to you,"

01:09:46.330 --> 01:09:51.259
but now the internet empowers this practice
and you can

01:09:51.259 --> 01:09:53.920
share with only people who are in certain
groups or

01:09:53.920 --> 01:09:54.920
everybody and there is these platforms now
where the

01:09:54.920 --> 01:09:56.489
internet is touching that a little bit, in
a sense that it's

01:09:56.489 --> 01:10:00.679
not happening on the internet, it's happening
in people's

01:10:00.679 --> 01:10:03.530
houses where they pack all the boxes and print
out the

01:10:03.530 --> 01:10:04.530
labels, but internet is ubiquitously letting
this happen.

01:10:04.530 --> 01:10:05.530
Things like thredUP, things like Getaround
or RelayRides,

01:10:05.530 --> 01:10:06.560
which are kind of like the AirBNB for cars
so there is this

01:10:06.560 --> 01:10:08.260
collaborative consumption again through the
mobile device,

01:10:08.260 --> 01:10:13.130
allowing the internet to go through this whole
platform and

01:10:13.130 --> 01:10:21.780
enable us to help enough people go in a web
page in surfing

01:10:21.780 --> 01:10:26.360
around and have a social experience. I think
those type of

01:10:26.360 --> 01:10:27.360
marketplaces that are inherently social, that
are happening

01:10:27.360 --> 01:10:28.360
offline are really interesting from an investment
perspective, because A) They are marketplaces

01:10:28.360 --> 01:10:30.830
that are
really stable and hard to dislodge overtime

01:10:30.830 --> 01:10:34.130
but B) Taking
behavior that is just really has been happening

01:10:34.130 --> 01:10:38.760
for a long
time and making it more powerful and more

01:10:38.760 --> 01:10:41.040
extensible in lots
of ways.

01:10:41.040 --> 01:10:45.600
I think that's one things that's been really
exciting. There

01:10:45.600 --> 01:10:51.250
is other themes too but I think that's kind
of the next

01:10:51.250 --> 01:10:55.400
generation of social e-commerce.

01:10:55.400 --> 01:11:01.830
I saw Brian, you were smiling when he was
saying AirBNB of

01:11:01.830 --> 01:11:05.800
something and that means you really know you've
made it when

01:11:05.800 --> 01:11:06.989
people are saying that.

01:11:06.989 --> 01:11:10.261
When we launched we said we were the E-Bay
of space. It's

01:11:10.261 --> 01:11:24.510
funny how we came up with that name. We did
"why common air"

01:11:24.510 --> 01:11:28.900
it doesn't like to call itself incubation,
I guess you could

01:11:28.900 --> 01:11:31.110
say it's just kind of seed program where they
give you

01:11:31.110 --> 01:11:33.010
$20000 and it has a bit of collegial field;
there is

01:11:33.010 --> 01:11:34.010
typically 20 or 30 companies that will enter
twice a year to

01:11:34.010 --> 01:11:35.010
this 12-week program. Every week there is
a notable speaker

01:11:35.010 --> 01:11:36.010
and this all leads to this final day called
"Demo Day".

01:11:36.010 --> 01:11:37.230
Leading up to Demo Day, we were trying to
explain what we

01:11:37.230 --> 01:11:38.670
were and I remember Paul Graham, people at
the time couldn't

01:11:38.670 --> 01:11:42.550
get their heads around if there was a real
business here and

01:11:42.550 --> 01:12:01.389
there is notably like couch surfing. A lot
of people

01:12:01.389 --> 01:12:09.900
originally thought we were couch surfing.
Today we have a

01:12:09.900 --> 01:12:13.389
lot of homes, we have like really big spaces,
we have

01:12:13.389 --> 01:12:15.790
castles, we even have cars and boats and all
sorts of

01:12:15.790 --> 01:12:17.310
spaces, but back then it was mostly bedrooms
and airbeds in

01:12:17.310 --> 01:12:18.700
living rooms. Paul Graham says, "You should
say you're the

01:12:18.700 --> 01:12:19.880
E-Bay of space," because E-Bay was a marketplace.
They were

01:12:19.880 --> 01:12:20.880
really big, they were a billion dollar company
and never

01:12:20.880 --> 01:12:21.880
rent space.

01:12:21.880 --> 01:12:27.240
We were so matter of fact and like, "Yeah,
we're the E-Bay

01:12:27.240 --> 01:12:42.860
of space," and at the time, I don't know if
we really could

01:12:42.860 --> 01:12:50.850
say we were because we were pretty much the
E-Bay of

01:12:50.850 --> 01:12:54.110
bedrooms but now we really have moved into
any type of

01:12:54.110 --> 01:12:55.110
unique space and the thing we'd like to say
is E-Bay can

01:12:55.110 --> 01:12:56.110
become a billion dollar company but monetizing
stuff in your

01:12:56.110 --> 01:12:57.429
home, how big could a company be if they monetized
the home,

01:12:57.429 --> 01:12:59.320
which is basically 25% of the annual assets
and the

01:12:59.320 --> 01:13:00.320
backyard, the front yard, all that kind of
space and one

01:13:00.320 --> 01:13:01.320
other thing maybe I'll say and this is to
follow up Brett's

01:13:01.320 --> 01:13:02.320
point was, I think in many ways social commerce
is the way

01:13:02.320 --> 01:13:03.320
things always were and the way that they will
be and I think

01:13:03.320 --> 01:13:04.320
it was kind of post World War too, there was
this period of

01:13:04.320 --> 01:13:05.320
maybe 60 years, at least in our space, where
it wasn't like

01:13:05.320 --> 01:13:06.320
that and the story goes like this:

01:13:06.320 --> 01:13:07.320
We came up with a concept; I told my mom about
it. My mom is

01:13:07.320 --> 01:13:08.320
59 years old, baby boomer, and she thought
we were crazy and

01:13:08.320 --> 01:13:10.150
most of her friends probably thought we were
crazy. And I

01:13:10.150 --> 01:13:12.139
told my late grandfather about it. My grandfather
grew up in

01:13:12.139 --> 01:13:14.230
Great Depression and he said, "Of course,"
and I go, "What

01:13:14.230 --> 01:13:18.410
do you mean, of course?" He had done this
when he was

01:13:18.410 --> 01:13:22.400
growing up. Of course there was no internet,
but there

01:13:22.400 --> 01:13:26.450
weren't modern hotels like we know today.
People would stay

01:13:26.450 --> 01:13:35.040
in bed and breakfasts and inns or in private
residences. The

01:13:35.040 --> 01:13:40.489
word "Southern hospitality" really means like
taking people

01:13:40.489 --> 01:13:42.260
into your homes and they have that symbol,
a pineapple,

01:13:42.260 --> 01:13:43.260
which is a symbol of hospitality. They used
to have

01:13:43.260 --> 01:13:44.260
pineapples at the ends of your bed.

01:13:44.260 --> 01:13:45.260
In other words, this is how people have been
traveling since

01:13:45.260 --> 01:13:46.260
the biblical times. A hotel 100 years ago
looked like a bed

01:13:46.260 --> 01:13:47.260
and breakfast today; a bed and breakfast 100
years ago

01:13:47.260 --> 01:13:48.260
looked like AirBNB today and so, it was really
in 1960 like

01:13:48.260 --> 01:13:50.250
the Marriott or Hilton had started really
taking off, so it'

01:13:50.250 --> 01:13:51.250
s this idea that we're the new way is kind
of true, but at

01:13:51.250 --> 01:13:53.840
the same time it's what people were doing
for a really long

01:13:53.840 --> 01:13:54.840
period of time.

01:13:54.840 --> 01:13:57.690
I think for a period of time there was this
idea that

01:13:57.690 --> 01:14:00.000
strangers were being connected from so many
different places

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:02.830
and there was this absence of trust and reputation
in any

01:14:02.830 --> 01:14:05.650
kind of currency. I think finally all those
things came back

01:14:05.650 --> 01:14:07.969
together and now you're seeing all these things
happen.

01:14:07.969 --> 01:14:23.280
And it feels like it's a rich space for a
lot of different

01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:26.320
types of startups actually to start popping
up which is why

01:14:26.320 --> 01:14:28.710
we're seeing sort of the AirBNB of lots of
different types

01:14:28.710 --> 01:14:29.710
of companies.

01:14:29.710 --> 01:14:30.949
I just want to add one thing to that because
it's an

01:14:30.949 --> 01:14:36.650
interesting point related to the fact that
this is how

01:14:36.650 --> 01:14:37.650
things used to be. I think a lot of these
social businesses

01:14:37.650 --> 01:14:38.650
can be proven out by entrepreneurs at an early
stage without

01:14:38.650 --> 01:14:39.650
any technology at all, so one really interesting
example of

01:14:39.650 --> 01:14:40.650
that is the Rent-a-Runway story where Jenn
and Jenny had

01:14:40.650 --> 01:14:41.650
this idea to be the Netflix of kind of aspirational
dresses.

01:14:41.650 --> 01:14:42.650
Instead of buying a $1500 dress, you can rent
that dress for

01:14:42.650 --> 01:14:47.050
$100 through Rent-a-Way and they'll send it
to your house.

01:14:47.050 --> 01:14:48.050
They had this idea coming out of Harvard Business
School and

01:14:48.050 --> 01:14:49.050
when they met with us, we basically said,
"Prove it," and

01:14:49.050 --> 01:14:50.050
they said "We'll have to hire all these engineers,
we have

01:14:50.050 --> 01:14:51.050
to build this front end, we have to build
this back end,"

01:14:51.050 --> 01:14:52.050
and we said, "No, no. Don't do that. You pick
the venue,"

01:14:52.050 --> 01:14:53.050
but they decided to do it at sorority parties
at Yale,

01:14:53.050 --> 01:14:54.050
Harvard, Princeton and they showed up at the
sorority

01:14:54.050 --> 01:14:55.050
parties. In advance, they had sent out invitations
to rent

01:14:55.050 --> 01:14:56.050
dresses.

01:14:56.050 --> 01:14:57.050
They showed up with a small inventory of dresses
just to

01:14:57.050 --> 01:14:58.050
test to see if people would pay money to rent
the dresses,

01:14:58.050 --> 01:14:59.050
what would happen to a dress at a sorority
party, how it

01:14:59.050 --> 01:15:00.050
came back, could it be re-used, lots of things
they learned

01:15:00.050 --> 01:15:01.050
without any technology at all.

01:15:01.050 --> 01:15:02.050
I think a lot of these social proof points
can really prove

01:15:02.050 --> 01:15:03.050
it out, because I think the technology is
what lets it scale

01:15:03.050 --> 01:15:06.139
massively but the technology is usually never
the business

01:15:06.139 --> 01:15:09.320
in itself, so I think that was really an interesting
anecdote and I think it applies to a lot of

01:15:09.320 --> 01:15:10.320
social commerce
businesses.

01:15:10.320 --> 01:15:11.320
Yeah, we actually have a very similar story
with Chegg.

01:15:11.320 --> 01:15:12.320
Chegg came from a pivot out of Craigslist
for colleges

01:15:12.320 --> 01:15:13.320
basically and Facebook came out with Craigslist
and so we

01:15:13.320 --> 01:15:14.320
were like, we can't really be Craigslist for
colleges

01:15:14.320 --> 01:15:15.320
anymore. They had this idea everyone is exchanging
textbooks

01:15:15.320 --> 01:15:16.320
on this site and so, let's try to pivot there.

01:15:16.320 --> 01:15:17.320
Instead of creating a whole new site with
the Chegg brand on

01:15:17.320 --> 01:15:18.320
it, they created a site called Textbookflix.
And then, what

01:15:18.320 --> 01:15:19.320
we did was we got an Amazon prime count and
kids would come

01:15:19.320 --> 01:15:20.320
in; I think we had 1000 students up first
quarter, come in,

01:15:20.320 --> 01:15:21.320
try to rent a textbook. We'd buy it off of
Amazon and ship

01:15:21.320 --> 01:15:22.320
it for free out to these students and they
would call and

01:15:22.320 --> 01:15:23.320
say, "I just a box from Amazon," we're like,
"Yeah, just

01:15:23.320 --> 01:15:24.320
send it back to us when you're done."

01:15:24.320 --> 01:15:25.320
It was extraordinarily successful from that
perspective. We

01:15:25.320 --> 01:15:26.320
got to test out exactly as was mentioned here
by Brett;

01:15:26.320 --> 01:15:27.320
whether or not students would return the textbooks,
what

01:15:27.320 --> 01:15:28.320
state did they return it in and being able
to test all that

01:15:28.320 --> 01:15:29.320
hypothesis out before really even launching
the business was

01:15:29.320 --> 01:15:30.320
extraordinarily valuable, so I think that's
very much true

01:15:30.320 --> 01:15:31.320
for a lot of these types of businesses as
it was proven out

01:15:31.320 --> 01:15:32.320
by AirBNB.

01:15:32.320 --> 01:15:33.320
Another question that I'm going back to One
Kings Lane, it's

01:15:33.320 --> 01:15:56.130
a little bit of a different type of business
with AirBNB but

01:15:56.130 --> 01:15:57.130
I wanted to go back to this notion that you
were just

01:15:57.130 --> 01:15:58.130
talking about how you would use your community
actually to

01:15:58.130 --> 01:15:59.130
help inform some of the back end of your business,
and I

01:15:59.130 --> 01:16:00.130
think that's a really interesting concept
because it feels

01:16:00.130 --> 01:16:01.130
like social commerce then what you're saying
is not just

01:16:01.130 --> 01:16:02.130
people coming and buying stuff, it's actually
some of the

01:16:02.130 --> 01:16:03.130
information that they are providing you to
help you optimize

01:16:03.130 --> 01:16:04.130
your business.

01:16:04.130 --> 01:16:05.130
I'd love to hear a little bit more about that.

01:16:05.130 --> 01:16:06.130
Sure. What One Kings Lane is trying to do
over the next year

01:16:06.130 --> 01:16:07.130
or so is more of our merchandising and we
do it all

01:16:07.130 --> 01:16:08.130
ourselves. It's beautiful, it's very expensive
to do, it

01:16:08.130 --> 01:16:09.130
looks great, but we want our consumers to
do the

01:16:09.130 --> 01:16:10.130
merchandising, so more user generated content
because I can

01:16:10.130 --> 01:16:11.130
tell a story and I can paint a picture of
the room; if I can

01:16:11.130 --> 01:16:12.130
get a consumer to do the same thing, it's
much more

01:16:12.130 --> 01:16:13.130
powerful, it's much more authentic, it's much
more real. And

01:16:13.130 --> 01:16:14.130
so, overtime the site will come more from
our consumers than

01:16:14.130 --> 01:16:15.130
merchandise and the aspiration.

01:16:15.130 --> 01:16:16.130
All the ideas will come from them because
consumers actually

01:16:16.130 --> 01:16:17.130
have more trust in other consumers who share
like-minded

01:16:17.130 --> 01:16:18.130
interests than they do in folks like us. They
love our

01:16:18.130 --> 01:16:19.130
stuff, they trust people who are like them
and so, more and

01:16:19.130 --> 01:16:20.130
more the look and the feel, the merchandise
that appears in

01:16:20.130 --> 01:16:21.130
the site, the recommendations you name it,
will come from

01:16:21.130 --> 01:16:22.130
our consumer base and we think that's very
powerful.

01:16:22.130 --> 01:16:23.130
And again it goes back to the notion that
no one has really

01:16:23.130 --> 01:16:24.130
cracked the notion of amazing content and
an amazing content

01:16:24.130 --> 01:16:25.130
woven it together to provide context that
really inspires,

01:16:25.130 --> 01:16:26.130
surprises and delights consumers and I think
that's the next

01:16:26.130 --> 01:16:27.130
wave you're going to see and again, it's not
math. It's math

01:16:27.130 --> 01:16:28.130
and editorial woven together and we think
the editorial from

01:16:28.130 --> 01:16:29.130
our consumers is much more powerful.

01:16:29.130 --> 01:16:30.130
Great. And then Brett, going back to then
the concept of

01:16:30.130 --> 01:16:31.130
Groupon, it feels like there is just with
the wild success

01:16:31.130 --> 01:16:32.130
of Groupon there has been billion other Groupon
clones that

01:16:32.130 --> 01:16:33.130
have been born. How, as a company, do you
stay ahead of

01:16:33.130 --> 01:16:34.130
that? Do you think that there is room for
everyone or as

01:16:34.130 --> 01:16:35.130
there is more competition, does that create
problems for a

01:16:35.130 --> 01:16:36.130
business like Groupon? How do you guys think
about that?

01:16:36.130 --> 01:16:37.130
There has definitely been a lot of companies
that have

01:16:37.130 --> 01:16:38.130
pivoted into this space whether they were
in it to begin

01:16:38.130 --> 01:16:39.130
with and there are companies such Yelp or
even Google kind

01:16:39.130 --> 01:16:40.130
of coming in and try to be in this space.
I think, to us,

01:16:40.130 --> 01:16:41.130
that's a sort of flattery I guess in some
sense, because the

01:16:41.130 --> 01:16:42.130
company has done so well. I think the thing
we hear from the

01:16:42.130 --> 01:16:43.130
merchant standpoint that's very interesting
is that as a

01:16:43.130 --> 01:16:44.130
merchant now locally, you get calls from countless
daily

01:16:44.130 --> 01:16:45.130
deals sites all the time and so while some
of the fatigue on

01:16:45.130 --> 01:16:46.130
the user standpoint for how many emails can
you actually

01:16:46.130 --> 01:16:47.130
subscribe to, daily deal emails, there is
sort of a question

01:16:47.130 --> 01:16:48.130
there. It doesn't take that long to read an
email.

01:16:48.130 --> 01:16:49.130
On the merchant side, to engage with a salesperson
from ten

01:16:49.130 --> 01:16:50.130
different daily deals sites, that can be really
tiring. I

01:16:50.130 --> 01:16:51.130
think there is an interesting dynamic going
on where there

01:16:51.130 --> 01:16:52.130
are maybe 100 copycat companies in the Bay
Area, they are

01:16:52.130 --> 01:16:53.130
all calling on the same merchants we want
to work with and

01:16:53.130 --> 01:16:54.130
that we have worked with. To a certain extent,
these

01:16:54.130 --> 01:16:55.130
merchants they want to work with us because
they need new

01:16:55.130 --> 01:16:56.130
customers, but they need to be dedicating
99% of their time

01:16:56.130 --> 01:16:57.130
to running their business and making sure
that that's

01:16:57.130 --> 01:16:58.130
profitable and making sure that that's running
well.

01:16:58.130 --> 01:16:59.130
A daily deal site for them is a way to acquire
new customers

01:16:59.130 --> 01:17:00.130
that are loyal, that come back in the door
time and time

01:17:00.130 --> 01:17:01.130
again, but they can't spend too much time
working with us,

01:17:01.130 --> 01:17:02.130
we need to make it really easy for them. And
so, I think the

01:17:02.130 --> 01:17:03.130
more options there are for them, the more
actually it helps

01:17:03.130 --> 01:17:04.130
us to a certain extent and more it helps some
of the leaders

01:17:04.130 --> 01:17:05.130
in the space that have larger subscriber lists,
that are at

01:17:05.130 --> 01:17:06.130
a greater scale than some of the people who
have looked at

01:17:06.130 --> 01:17:07.130
the model and said, "That's so easy. I just
need to start

01:17:07.130 --> 01:17:08.130
selling local businesses," and then quickly
learned while

01:17:08.130 --> 01:17:09.130
there is a network in fact here, it's just
localized. It's

01:17:09.130 --> 01:17:10.130
not the same network effect that's at play
at E-Bay, it's

01:17:10.130 --> 01:17:11.130
all within San Francisco, it's all within
Palo Alto, it's

01:17:11.130 --> 01:17:12.130
all within Chicago.

01:17:12.130 --> 01:17:13.130
So I think there is some dynamics out there
that some people

01:17:13.130 --> 01:17:14.130
talk about but don't really think through
all the way that I

01:17:14.130 --> 01:17:15.130
think will contribute to having scale as an
advantage for us

01:17:15.130 --> 01:17:16.130
across multiple marketplaces.

01:17:16.130 --> 01:17:17.130
But if each merchant is getting 10-15 calls
from every other

01:17:17.130 --> 01:17:18.130
coupon site, does that make it a race to the
bottom in terms

01:17:18.130 --> 01:17:19.130
of margins for companies like yours or is
it one or two

01:17:19.130 --> 01:17:20.130
survivors will really emerge in terms of we
have a big

01:17:20.130 --> 01:17:21.130
subscriber and so therefore that's what we
offer?

01:17:21.130 --> 01:17:22.130
I think the subscriber base is the key thing
there. For

01:17:22.130 --> 01:17:23.130
them, again, I think that's it's more about
I want to be

01:17:23.130 --> 01:17:24.130
able to get the right number of customers
in and in the

01:17:24.130 --> 01:17:25.130
right cadence and it's less about the margin
itself. These

01:17:25.130 --> 01:17:26.130
merchants are ones that again, they can operate,
they know

01:17:26.130 --> 01:17:27.130
they operate good businesses, they know once
they get

01:17:27.130 --> 01:17:28.130
someone in the door that they can convince
them to come back

01:17:28.130 --> 01:17:29.130
in.

01:17:29.130 --> 01:17:30.130
A lot of times, a very local merchant, so
it's not as much

01:17:30.130 --> 01:17:31.130
like a Taco Bell where the person that's serving
them doesn'

01:17:31.130 --> 01:17:32.130
t get to know that customer. They take pride
in every

01:17:32.130 --> 01:17:33.130
customer that comes in their door. They want
to get to know

01:17:33.130 --> 01:17:34.130
them, that's where that social aspect - it's
funny you were

01:17:34.130 --> 01:17:35.130
mentioning how it used to be like - I think
in a lot of ways

01:17:35.130 --> 01:17:36.130
what we do socially is we give these local
merchants the

01:17:36.130 --> 01:17:37.130
ability to reconnect with their local consumers
in a way

01:17:37.130 --> 01:17:38.130
that hasn't been seen in the United States
in a long time.

01:17:38.130 --> 01:17:39.130
I lived in Spain for a year in 2004 and I
became a local at

01:17:39.130 --> 01:17:40.130
a bar and I'd never done that in America,
I'd never gotten

01:17:40.130 --> 01:17:41.130
to know someone that actually served me food
and gave me

01:17:41.130 --> 01:17:42.130
drinks and we were friends and that's for
us the ultimate

01:17:42.130 --> 01:17:43.130
success for Groupon, can we create that sort
of dynamic

01:17:43.130 --> 01:17:44.130
through the internet, through mobile in local
basis?

01:17:44.130 --> 01:17:45.130
Just to tie that all back, I think that this
social

01:17:45.130 --> 01:17:46.130
discussion one massive barrier I think that
Groupon can have

01:17:46.130 --> 01:17:47.130
at scale is that they can push social promotions
that are

01:17:47.130 --> 01:17:48.130
curated because of your social graph and basically
drive

01:17:48.130 --> 01:17:49.130
better the right consumers to right merchants
and the right

01:17:49.130 --> 01:17:50.130
deals to right consumers in a way that once
you have that

01:17:50.130 --> 01:17:51.130
scale upstarts can't do that well and I think
that's

01:17:51.130 --> 01:17:52.130
definitely one really exciting thing about
once you hit

01:17:52.130 --> 01:17:53.130
scale doing Groupon that other people can't
touch you, so I

01:17:53.130 --> 01:17:54.130
don't know if you guys have been proven out
yet but I'm sure

01:17:54.130 --> 01:17:55.130
you're working on it.

01:17:55.130 --> 01:17:56.130
Data at scale in any business is a definite
advantage and

01:17:56.130 --> 01:17:57.130
having the resources is to take that data
and figure out

01:17:57.130 --> 01:17:58.130
what it means and then apply it to your product
then that's

01:17:58.130 --> 01:17:59.130
something that we think about a lot, we put
a lot of effort

01:17:59.130 --> 01:18:00.130
on.

01:18:00.130 --> 01:18:01.130
Great. I wanted to gather some final thoughts
from each of

01:18:01.130 --> 01:18:02.130
you on where do you think this all goes over
the next, let's

01:18:02.130 --> 01:18:03.130
say, two to three years. It feels like social
commerce on

01:18:03.130 --> 01:18:04.130
some levels has reached a maturity in terms
of what it

01:18:04.130 --> 01:18:05.130
actually is but it feels like there is also
a lot more that

01:18:05.130 --> 01:18:06.130
it can actually do and there is a big promise
aspect to

01:18:06.130 --> 01:18:07.130
social commerce.

01:18:07.130 --> 01:18:08.130
Before we turn it over to the audience for
questions I'd

01:18:08.130 --> 01:18:09.130
love to get your perspectives on where this
is all going in

01:18:09.130 --> 01:18:10.130
the next two, three years. Start with Brian.

01:18:10.130 --> 01:18:11.130
I know a lot of entrepreneurs that they have
slide decks and

01:18:11.130 --> 01:18:12.130
in our slide deck we had a make up a giant
guess on the

01:18:12.130 --> 01:18:13.130
marketplace and we had to make a guess on
where this went

01:18:13.130 --> 01:18:14.130
and I'm pretty sure that a lot of people now
have our logos

01:18:14.130 --> 01:18:15.130
in their slide decks and they say, "I get
the Google alerts

01:18:15.130 --> 01:18:16.130
for AirBNB and every so often you'll see AirBNB
for cars,

01:18:16.130 --> 01:18:17.130
AirBNB for events, AirBNB for food, AirBNB
for tutoring," so

01:18:17.130 --> 01:18:18.130
we've proven this model now which is really
interesting.

01:18:18.130 --> 01:18:19.130
I think that now that we've proven this at
a certain scale,

01:18:19.130 --> 01:18:20.130
it's really opened up people's, the door of
their

01:18:20.130 --> 01:18:21.130
imagination to all the other ways you can
connect and I

01:18:21.130 --> 01:18:22.130
really think it's all these different industries.
I think

01:18:22.130 --> 01:18:23.130
for AirBNB, the one thing I can say with certainty
is that

01:18:23.130 --> 01:18:24.130
it's going to grow quite significantly geographically.
Our

01:18:24.130 --> 01:18:25.130
site was global from day one and the reason
we were global

01:18:25.130 --> 01:18:26.130
and it's a very different than Groupon; we're
local but we

01:18:26.130 --> 01:18:27.130
bring the locals together from around the
world. It's not an

01:18:27.130 --> 01:18:28.130
in-store community.

01:18:28.130 --> 01:18:29.130
Basically, we started in London, Paris, New
York. People

01:18:29.130 --> 01:18:30.130
travel this cities from tens of thousands
cities around the

01:18:30.130 --> 01:18:31.130
world and so this is natural that a network
effect that

01:18:31.130 --> 01:18:32.130
happened where they came from these thousands
of cities

01:18:32.130 --> 01:18:33.130
around the world, they booked, they stayed
in New York. When

01:18:33.130 --> 01:18:34.130
they go back to wherever they came from and
we remarketed to

01:18:34.130 --> 01:18:35.130
them essentially, that's all it was.

01:18:35.130 --> 01:18:36.130
They would say, "You had a great experience.
You should rent

01:18:36.130 --> 01:18:37.130
your place, you should tell other people about
it. Unless

01:18:37.130 --> 01:18:38.130
your homeless, by definition you have space.
You can rent on

01:18:38.130 --> 01:18:39.130
AirBNB." That was essentially what we had
said to them and

01:18:39.130 --> 01:18:40.130
it really started to grow and so, I think
this is something

01:18:40.130 --> 01:18:41.130
that's spreading internationally and even
with our platform,

01:18:41.130 --> 01:18:42.130
it's spreading into different categories.

01:18:42.130 --> 01:18:43.130
We see ourselves not limited to bedrooms or
even necessarily

01:18:43.130 --> 01:18:44.130
houses. We have many people renting all different
types of

01:18:44.130 --> 01:18:45.130
spaces. A few weird use cases that have been
facilitated

01:18:45.130 --> 01:18:46.130
today are, when people first move to a city
now, AirBNB is

01:18:46.130 --> 01:18:47.130
an incredibly common use case. Let's say you
had to go to DC

01:18:47.130 --> 01:18:48.130
next week; you just found out you got a job
and you're not

01:18:48.130 --> 01:18:49.130
finding an apartment on Craigslist from another
city through

01:18:49.130 --> 01:18:50.130
a different Craigslist when you came in toward
the

01:18:50.130 --> 01:18:51.130
apartments and you have to rush, that doesn't
really work.

01:18:51.130 --> 01:18:52.130
People use AirBNB now for neighborhood sampling.
I want to

01:18:52.130 --> 01:18:53.130
try out a neighborhood once a week and see
what it's like.

01:18:53.130 --> 01:18:54.130
That's really, really common. And there is
a lot unexpected

01:18:54.130 --> 01:18:55.130
things. People are even renting out other
people's homes for

01:18:55.130 --> 01:18:56.130
dinner parties, which I thought was unbelievable.
It's like

01:18:56.130 --> 01:18:57.130
why get a really nice house? Get a small house
and when you

01:18:57.130 --> 01:18:58.130
want to impress somebody, rent a big house
for the night.

01:18:58.130 --> 01:18:59.130
To some extent, I guess where I'm going is
we think that

01:18:59.130 --> 01:19:00.130
platform allows people to project what they
want onto it, so

01:19:00.130 --> 01:19:01.130
the future for AirBNB and for these marketplaces
is if we

01:19:01.130 --> 01:19:02.130
create really dynamic platforms, people are
going to find

01:19:02.130 --> 01:19:03.130
